you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's program  residents and tourists in athens are  evacuated as wildfires continue to burn  across europe  we'll analyze the current intensity of  heat waves and what that could mean for  wildfires in the uk  in the future plus the prime minister  has been told he is missing an action on  climate change as he visits scotland  ahead of the crucial climate summit and  the petrol price rise which could make  shift to electric come sooner than we  thought  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we  track the changes happening to our world  right now and meet those  coming up with the solutions straight to  europe now where wildfires are still  burning and forcing people out of their  homes  for a second day in greece acres of land  have been scorched  near the capital athens in a moment  we'll have a look at what these extreme  temperatures  could mean in the uk in turkey  fires are burning for an eighth day and  there is growing anger with the  government accused of failing to get a  grip on fires affecting parts of the  country  firefighters are cutting back trees to  stop their spread  the fires have been made worse by  unusually hot temperatures low humidity  and high winds and thousands have been  forced to evacuate  athens in greece where firefighters are  tackling huge fires in the north of the  city  the blaze has been made worse by what  greek authorities are calling the worst  heat wave in decades  with temperatures in parts of the  country reaching 46.3 degrees  celsius let's go to greece now and speak  to europe correspondent michelle  clifford she's in an area on the  outskirts of athens  um michelle hello to you what's  happening where you are  well there's been dozens of fires across  greece over the last  couple of days but the area around here  saw at the biggest  blazes we're about 20 kilometers from uh  athens city  and the blaze surrounded a village  called vari  bobby where i'm standing uh now now  residents were evacuated and when they  come back they're going to see some  really  quite horrific scenes this house behind  me is where a  95 year old woman and her 98 year old  husband lived it's completely uh  destroyed as  is the land if we look all around the  property everything  is completely scorched now this was a  family plot  their son lived in this house over here  that is incredibly badly damaged i spoke  to him earlier and he said  they were in their property yesterday  around lunchtime and they could see the  fires and they seem to be  a long way in the distance and then he  said within 10 minutes the fires were  on top of them and they had to drive  through the flames to get out now  800 firefighters overnight tried to  tackle  the blazes they were very intense today  they seem to have got most of the big  flames out the big problem though now  is that the whole landscape around here  is smoldering there is the potential  potential for more fires uh to ignite  and turn into something  really massive all over again okay  michelle thank you  well let's take a look at what's  happening in europe the south of europe  is usually the worst affected by  wildfires with countries like  spain seeing a weekly average of 80  fires in july between 2008 and 2020  but 2021 has been especially bad for the  region particularly  for countries like france and turkey  since the start of the summer countries  in southern europe have seen a surge in  the number of wildfires compared to the  same period in previous  years in italy for example at the start  of august the figures were already  double the average but the environmental  impact has been most pronounced for  turkey  which saw over a hundred and fifty  thousand hectares of land burnt at the  start of august  that is more than nine times the average  that was seen  between 2008 and 2020.  so could we be seeing similar scenes in  the uk well the june 2018  saddle worth more fires were some of the  largest uk wildfires on record and  lasted  for about three weeks at their peak they  covered an area of four thousand four  hundred and eighty acres of moorland  in 2019 there were fires in both maury  and keith ness in scotland that scorched  about  29 000 acres in northern ireland there  have been more than a thousand wildfires  in the mourne mountains over the last  five years and in april this year a huge  wildfire on those mountains was declared  a major incident  damaging 494 acres of land  let's speak to professor stephen durr  who's the director at the center for  wildfire research at swansea university  professor thank you so much for talking  to us  here on the climate show so what do you  think do you think we could see  similar fires here that we're seeing in  europe and turkey  well unfortunately not quite because  most of the fires we have in the uk  uh affect moorland grassland and  shrubland area so these fires tend not  to be so severe but sometimes we do have  forest fires occurring  and whilst we not yet have those extreme  weather conditions that we see in the  mediterranean  they're certainly becoming more severe  all the climate models  are in agreement that in the future we  will get more severe fire weather which  make  forest fires with big flames like we see  there more likely  and what would happen if we did see  fires like that  here well these fires as we've seen in  the media are extremely difficult to  control so we should do everything to  avoid  um extreme forest fires well what are we  doing if  are we mitigating in any way so that we  don't see the same situation here  well there are a number of things we can  do fortunately in the uk because nearly  all fires  here are human caused that could be by  awesome arson that could be by accident  and we can reduce the chances of those  ignitions we can also manage our fuels  more intensely so we can remove dead and  dry vegetation  in the landscape either manually or  using controlled fires which will  have a strong effect in reducing fire  risk  and is that being done not yet in the uk  uh in terms of let's say at larger  scales  but there are experiments and small  scale approaches in the uk where  prescribed fire for example  is used to reduce the flammability of  the landscape  under conditions when fires are not  extreme so in in the spring or  late in the autumn and this will then  help reduce the risk  uh during the summer and later in the  spring when the fuels can be very dry  okay professor thank you so much for  talking to us and staying with wildfires  in california a fire that has been  burning since june has intensified in  the last 24 hours  evacuations were ordered for about a  thousand people because of the dixie  fire  that's grown to over 395 square miles  across the plumas and butte counties in  northern california  so let's take you over to our data  dashboard now here at the top  is the real time powermix in the uk and  you can see renewables make up 38  and fossil fuels 43 and that of course  has an impact on global temperatures  this shows the human-induced global  warming  since the industrial revolution and it  is ticking up the whole time  the aim agreed by 196 parties at the un  cop 21 summit in paris in 2015 is to  keep this number  under 2 degrees preferably to 1.5  degrees of warming  this well this is the time left to that  crucial  1.5 degrees right to some other news on  climate change now and the national  oceanic and atmospheric administration  has issued its hurricane season forecast  for 2021 they've predicted  that the likelihood of an above normal  2021  atlantic hurricane season is 65  with 15 to 21 named storms  scientists warned that warmer ocean  temperatures and higher sea levels will  make hurricanes more powerful and  destructive  posing a significant risk to public  health and to human lives  reusing and repairing household goods  could create more than 450 000 jobs  across the uk by 2035  the think tank green alliance found that  roles in engineering  machinery repair and administration  could be created while curbing the uk's  unsustainable throwaway culture jobs in  manufacturing are predicted to decline  but researchers say  funding redesign and repair initiatives  could ensure  future employment in the sector and  finally a study has found that 98  of emperor penguin colonies are in  danger of extinction by the end of the  century  if the antarctic continues to melt at  its current rate  that's because the flightless species  depend on sea ice to breed  and raise their young but the species  decline can be halted if warming is kept  to under 1.5 degrees celsius  compared to pre-industrial temperatures  well cop26 will be held in glasgow in  november and ahead of that boris johnson  is visiting renewable energy projects in  scotland  while on a two-day visit from today but  the opposition leader sakirshama has  already accused the prime minister of  being  off track to meet his own climate  targets  we need a strategic plan to enable us  to transition whether that's cars  whether it's energy  whether it's wind turbines here in  scotland hugely  important a strategic plan and what  we've seen  from the snp here and from the uk  government  is lots of targets lots of ambitions not  matched  by reality well let's speak to our  political correspondent joe pike  who's in fife um joe pressure on the pm  while he's up in scotland  certainly kimberly there is pressure on  boris johnson both  the prime minister and the labour leader  are here on two day trips  and remarkably similar in focus kirsten  nature reserve today  in glasgow talking about the run-up to  the cop  climate conference in that city  scotland's biggest  in november boris johnson today is  actually meeting policing leaders but  that has a  climate change link too because of  course the  force police scotland will be mounting  that massive security operation  around the conference protecting the  heads of state and heads of government  attending it it's tomorrow where boris  johnson will be visiting i understand  uh more climate change linked projects  saying uh the government is investing in  renewable energy  and in green technology as well  i think all of this does show that this  issue  has risen up the political agenda a  research  published recently suggests that the  biggest risk that  voters think that the uk faces is after  the pandemic climate change and  therefore they want their politicians  to explain how they are planning to  tackle it okay joe thank you  well one way to reach those climate  emissions is the shift from petrol to  electric vehicles and today it's being  warned that petrol prices have reached  an  eight year high in the uk but does a  rise in fuel costs  outweigh the upfront cost of an electric  car our business correspondent handling  smith has been looking into this  she joins us now so helena are the  rising prices going to have an effect  well it's really difficult to tell  kimberly and it's difficult to tell  because there's a lot of complicated  factors  going into this firstly that very high  price we're seeing at the petrol pump at  the moment it won't necessarily last  uh it's come about because of a variety  of complicated reasons but  problems at every stage in the supply  chain because of the pandemic from the  oil rigs all the way to the petrol  stations  and also a classic supply and demand  problem at the beginning of the pandemic  no one was driving and flying so much  the all-producing countries produced  less  uh now the economy's reopened everybody  wants more there isn't so much to go  around the prices go up so  that may not be sustained the other  reason it may not necessarily push  people towards electric  is of course what we always talk about  isn't it the cost of electric vehicles  because  even though an electric car is cheaper  to run once you've got  it that big upfront cost is still there  for people  and if you're the sort of family or the  sort of individual who's struggling for  an extra 20p or so  uh on the mile at the petrol pump it's  unlikely you'll be able to  uh foot that big bill for an electric  car now groups like the rac  they're saying that the government and  others should use this moment as a  big incentive uh to try and push more  people over the edge who were perhaps  already considering electric this is  what they had to say  perhaps there is an opportunity now to  really capitalize on the the lower rates  that  ev drivers can you know can enjoy and  get more people into those  but the answer really is around kind of  flooding the market with new electric  vehicles  we know at the rac that most drivers  still buy their cars  second or third hand so the only way  those drivers are gonna be able to  afford electric ones  is if there's enough supply at the top  well that's everything from us  today on tomorrow's show green gaming  how developers are using games to  inspire players to make changes to their  own lives  thank you for watching see you then  [Music]

